#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097629} :activity/id #uuid "3dec8fd5-96f5-5d03-a211-5d2ff3da55fc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050155 :topic/id #uuid "3274c28d-4be4-51fc-9211-e22afa89f69d" :topic/math-academy-id 1088} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097801 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the sequence $a_n$ is defined as\n\n$$\na_n =  3\\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r = \\dfrac12.$ What is the behavior of this sequence as $n\\to\\infty?$\n\nLet's start by calculating the first few terms of the sequence:\n\n$$\na_1 = \\dfrac32, \\qquad a_2 = \\dfrac34, \\qquad a_3 = \\dfrac38, \\qquad a_4 = \\dfrac{3}{16}, \\qquad \\ldots\\,.\n$$\n\nPlotting the few terms of the sequence, we get a graph like the one below:\n\n![](images/8b/8b31b82126b56226a74aac5f3406d2d4ad5c85ec5c468e089acd028eba6c0469.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, it appears that $a_n\\to 0$ as $n\\to\\infty.$ This is indeed true.\n\nBecause $a_n$ approaches zero as $n\\to \\infty,$ we say that the sequence is **convergent** and that it **converges to **$0.$ We also say that the **limit of the sequence** is $0.$\n\nThe sequence is convergent because $|r| < 1,$ which causes the terms of the sequence to decrease to zero as $n$ increases." :tutorial/id #uuid "c977d786-2e9c-5b46-9aae-5025af2b3da6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1149 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e2fca69c-5a2d-5ac6-a30b-3592861d6d4e" :step/math-academy-id 2262 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096551 :question/id #uuid "bc0f8f02-ba1c-59c7-ba27-cb2148f2a9f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-646"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4380b34-0502-552b-b94f-e121eb855f1d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048829}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442210}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Convergence Behavior of Geometric Sequences Written Recursively"} :step/id #uuid "bd6fc951-0dda-51a1-aeb0-9e73b5e47429" :step/math-academy-id 2263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097879 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at the sequence $b_n,$ defined as\n\n$$\nb_n =  2^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r = 2.$ What is the behavior of this sequence as $n\\to\\infty?$\n\nLet's start by calculating the first few terms of the sequence:\n\n$$\nb_1 = 2, \\qquad b_2 = 4, \\qquad b_3 = 8, \\qquad b_4 = 16, \\qquad \\ldots\\,.\n$$\n\nPlotting the few terms of the sequence, we get a graph like the one below:\n\n![](images/62/629ac7251857a5c79d0648e3d68d13ebfce57d560c43af1c9662636d04b5702f.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, it appears that $b_n\\to \\infty$ as $n\\to\\infty.$ This is indeed true.\n\nBecause $b_n$ grows without bound as $n\\to \\infty,$ we say that the sequence is **divergent.** The sequence is divergent because $|r| > 1,$ which causes the terms of the sequence to increase as $n$ increases." :tutorial/id #uuid "63956297-e5ed-5ed5-9fd1-21167efbfab9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4865 :tutorial/title "Divergent Geometric Sequences"} :step/id #uuid "0590acbf-3f08-519e-8730-b102037d3717" :step/math-academy-id 30462 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097715}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097880 :tutorial/content "To summarize, suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <    1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent" :tutorial/id #uuid "545dc12e-bce3-5e0f-bc1e-62d444e9ba7a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4866 :tutorial/title "A Summary of Convergence Results For Geometric Sequences"} :step/id #uuid "e5664d35-ec55-5895-8884-65c6fdf62973" :step/math-academy-id 30464 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096795 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096604 :question/id #uuid "90ed1cce-a831-58b0-942a-d8b9a17583cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-9964"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a850ba4f-dbb5-5a09-afb0-3dc3db709420" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050977}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442208}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges"} :step/id #uuid "b420bd1a-a7ba-53a6-948c-f09c441c35f4" :step/math-academy-id 37514 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096605 :question/id #uuid "0bbb71a2-ff71-5f25-80bc-e56bbed9ac1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9989"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5986f765-9036-5124-a498-bd898119c9c5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050067}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442207}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the Nth Term"} :step/id #uuid "b7aaa5b4-33fb-5c5f-a0de-7d674061edc6" :step/math-academy-id 37628 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097900 :tutorial/content "Okay, so we've seen that a geometric sequence is convergent for $|r| < 1$ and divergent when $|r| >1.$ But what about when $|r| = 1?$\n\nWe have two cases:\n\n- When $r=1,$ the geometric sequence is convergent and converges to the first term. \n\nFor example, consider the sequence $c_n = 2(1)^n,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/6f/6f11903fd259e75a51de9877fadbb97e975b48bce229bad954aae2d6b3da36c9.png)\n\nIn this case, the sequence is convergent, and the limit of the sequence is $c_1 = 2.$\n\n- When $r=-1,$ the geometric sequence is divergent. \n\nFor example, consider the sequence $d_n = 2(-1)^n,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/3e/3e4de425d4e6af816380b275e536685f524adfb33643715b093d6def3762f03a.png)\n\nIn this case, the sequence oscillates between $2$ and $-2.$ It does not approach a fixed number, so it is divergent." :tutorial/id #uuid "1c43e3ce-7fc5-51e4-91e8-d598ab3fb1f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6223 :tutorial/title "Edge Cases"} :step/id #uuid "80e4b31c-2592-53cf-b150-6650033614f3" :step/math-academy-id 37798 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097675}}] :activity/title "Convergence of Geometric Sequences" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096877}]}
